1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,780
Hello!

2
00:00:00,780 --> 00:00:07,150
Last time we learned the eight simple vowels - ㅏ,

3
00:00:07,150 --> 00:00:09,750
ㅓ, ㅗ, ㅜ, ㅡ, ㅣ, ㅐ, ㅔ.

4
00:00:09,750 --> 00:00:14,950
Can you see me running up from far away?

5
00:00:14,950 --> 00:00:21,840
Today we'll walk right around this plaza as we learn.

6
00:00:25,720 --> 00:00:29,098
Today we'll build whole words from those vowels alone.

7
00:00:29,098 --> 00:00:30,536
Not a single consonant.

8
00:00:30,536 --> 00:00:32,663
Vowels only, no consonants at all.

9
00:00:32,663 --> 00:00:35,290
Hard to believe? Let me start with a flip.

10
00:00:35,290 --> 00:00:37,980
There are seven words waiting for us today.

11
00:00:44,220 --> 00:00:45,363
When we write,

12
00:00:45,363 --> 00:00:49,852
a small circle ㅇ sits in front - but it makes no sound.

13
00:00:49,852 --> 00:00:51,730
It just holds the seat.

14
00:00:53,770 --> 00:00:55,745
Shall we head out into the plaza?

15
00:00:55,745 --> 00:00:58,079
Today's words are hiding all around it.

16
00:00:58,079 --> 00:00:59,396
Each time we find one,

17
00:00:59,396 --> 00:01:01,670
let's say it out loud together. Ready?

18
00:01:07,350 --> 00:01:11,802
Our first word - 아이. It means a child.

19
00:01:11,802 --> 00:01:16,605
Just two vowels, ㅏ and ㅣ, and no batchim.

20
00:01:16,605 --> 00:01:21,877
Shall we sit on the steps and take it slowly?

21
00:01:21,877 --> 00:01:25,743
The 아 in 아이 opens the mouth wide,

22
00:01:25,743 --> 00:01:29,960
and the 이 pulls it out to the sides.

23
00:01:30,410 --> 00:01:34,916
Step down through it, one at a time. 아 - 이.

24
00:01:34,916 --> 00:01:38,374
Now together. 아이. Say it with me.

25
00:01:38,374 --> 00:01:40,680
One step is one sound.

26
00:01:40,680 --> 00:01:43,614
One step on 아, another on 이.

27
00:01:43,614 --> 00:01:50,950
Count the syllables one at a time like this and you can read any word.

28
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:55,407
Second - 이.

29
00:01:55,407 --> 00:02:01,546
A single ㅣ, lips pulled wide, is already a word.

30
00:02:01,546 --> 00:02:03,720
It means a tooth.

31
00:02:05,750 --> 00:02:08,629
Oh - something's rolling this way.

32
00:02:08,629 --> 00:02:12,440
Our third word: 오이, a crunchy green cucumber.

33
00:02:12,440 --> 00:02:14,641
Again two vowels, ㅗ and ㅣ.

34
00:02:14,641 --> 00:02:17,097
Cucumbers are juicy and cool,

35
00:02:17,097 --> 00:02:19,976
so we eat a lot of them in summer.

36
00:02:19,976 --> 00:02:22,940
Let me crouch down and pick one up.

37
00:02:22,940 --> 00:02:24,210
There - got it!

38
00:02:29,620 --> 00:02:36,119
Round your lips, then pull them wide. 오 - 이. 오이.

39
00:02:36,119 --> 00:02:42,077
Round your lips, then pull them wide. 오 - 이.

40
00:02:42,077 --> 00:02:46,816
Can you see the two shapes joining?

41
00:02:46,816 --> 00:02:49,660
Once more: 오 - 이. 오이.

42
00:02:54,290 --> 00:02:58,181
Careful - don't let that one fall!

43
00:02:58,181 --> 00:03:01,500
Fourth: 우유, sweet white milk.

44
00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:06,650
Both ㅜ and ㅠ push the lips forward and round.

45
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:11,175
Slowly now. 우 - 유. 우유.

46
00:03:11,175 --> 00:03:16,179
Once more, holding the carton. 우 - 유.

47
00:03:16,179 --> 00:03:22,940
Your lips push forward and stay there, don't they?

48
00:03:24,920 --> 00:03:28,006
아이 and 오이 - same ending, both end in 이.

49
00:03:28,006 --> 00:03:31,962
Change one sound in front and the meaning changes.

50
00:03:31,962 --> 00:03:34,257
Only the first sound changed,

51
00:03:34,257 --> 00:03:37,739
and yet the meaning is completely different.

52
00:03:37,739 --> 00:03:40,350
That's how much one sound can do.

53
00:03:41,980 --> 00:03:43,188
Whoa! Fifth - 오.

54
00:03:43,188 --> 00:03:46,511
The sound you make when you're surprised, 오!

55
00:03:46,511 --> 00:03:47,946
, is itself a word.

56
00:03:47,946 --> 00:03:55,950
It's the sound that comes out by itself when you're startled - so you already knew this word before today.

57
00:04:00,240 --> 00:04:02,987
And 오 also means the number five.

58
00:04:02,987 --> 00:04:05,651
One, two, three, four, five - 오!

59
00:04:05,651 --> 00:04:08,731
Shall we open our fingers one by one?

60
00:04:08,731 --> 00:04:11,061
One, two, three, four, five.

61
00:04:11,061 --> 00:04:13,808
All five fingers open - that's 오.

62
00:04:13,808 --> 00:04:18,220
It's a number and a cry of surprise at the same time.

63
00:04:31,870 --> 00:04:34,883
Someone's sitting on the bench. Sixth word - 아우,

64
00:04:34,883 --> 00:04:37,016
a warm word for a younger sibling.

65
00:04:37,016 --> 00:04:40,594
It's what an older brother or sister calls a younger one.

66
00:04:40,594 --> 00:04:42,728
You don't hear it much these days,

67
00:04:42,728 --> 00:04:45,050
but it turns up often in old stories.

68
00:04:50,630 --> 00:05:00,671
From ㅏ to ㅜ. Open wide, then round. 아 - 우. 아우.

69
00:05:00,671 --> 00:05:09,183
ㅏ opens wide, ㅜ rounds the lips. 아 - 우.

70
00:05:09,183 --> 00:05:15,295
My friend is copying me too.

71
00:05:15,295 --> 00:05:20,970
Try it with us: 아 - 우. 아우.

72
00:05:21,420 --> 00:05:25,909
Sounds have a temperature too.

73
00:05:25,909 --> 00:05:34,140
ㅏ and ㅗ are bright and warm; ㅓ and ㅜ are dark and cool.

74
00:05:34,590 --> 00:05:36,304
But ㅣ has no colour.

75
00:05:36,304 --> 00:05:38,960
Shaped after a standing person,

76
00:05:38,960 --> 00:05:42,303
it gets along with warm and cool alike.

77
00:05:42,303 --> 00:05:44,359
So ㅣ never takes a side.

78
00:05:44,359 --> 00:05:47,530
It just stands quietly in the middle.

79
00:05:50,810 --> 00:05:53,516
Shh... something's in the bush.

80
00:05:53,516 --> 00:05:57,443
Seventh word - 여우, the red fox of the forest.

81
00:05:57,443 --> 00:05:59,887
여 and 우 - vowels only again.

82
00:05:59,887 --> 00:06:02,942
The fox is famous for being clever,

83
00:06:02,942 --> 00:06:05,735
and turns up often in old tales.

84
00:06:05,735 --> 00:06:09,052
Let me creep up so I don't startle it.

85
00:06:09,052 --> 00:06:10,710
Shh, quietly now...

86
00:06:16,440 --> 00:06:20,863
Oh, it's gone. 여 - 우. 여우.

87
00:06:20,863 --> 00:06:26,171
여 is ㅣ sliding quickly into ㅓ.

88
00:06:26,171 --> 00:06:32,540
For now, just learn it as one sound.

89
00:06:32,990 --> 00:06:35,179
Same temperature,

90
00:06:35,179 --> 00:06:42,005
and they snap together like magnets - warm with warm,

91
00:06:42,005 --> 00:06:48,058
cool with cool. ㅗ and ㅏ make ㅘ; ㅜ and ㅓ make ㅝ.

92
00:06:48,058 --> 00:06:52,050
Warm with warm, cool with cool.

93
00:06:52,500 --> 00:07:03,735
Only ㅣ is the exception - it slips in on the warm side and the cool side alike.

94
00:07:03,735 --> 00:07:07,859
Like catching a falling leaf.

95
00:07:07,859 --> 00:07:15,111
Add ㅣ to ㅏ and you get ㅐ; add ㅣ to ㅓ and you get ㅔ.

96
00:07:15,111 --> 00:07:19,520
That's how a new sound is born.

97
00:07:19,970 --> 00:07:20,928
Now let's match.

98
00:07:20,928 --> 00:07:23,145
Look at the picture and say the word.

99
00:07:23,145 --> 00:07:25,361
The green vegetable? The white drink?

100
00:07:25,361 --> 00:07:27,338
The red animal? Did you get them?

101
00:07:27,338 --> 00:07:30,513
When you remember the picture and the sound together,

102
00:07:30,513 --> 00:07:32,310
it stays with you much longer.

103
00:07:37,290 --> 00:07:44,892
Let's gather today's seven words - 아이, 이, 오이, 우유,

104
00:07:44,892 --> 00:07:46,444
오, 아우, 여우.

105
00:07:46,444 --> 00:07:53,580
And one more: the place we spent all day - 야외,

106
00:07:53,580 --> 00:07:58,390
"outdoors". Vowels only, again!

107
00:08:00,780 --> 00:08:03,576
Practice today's words clearly in front of a mirror.

108
00:08:03,576 --> 00:08:05,028
When you can see the shape,

109
00:08:05,028 --> 00:08:06,265
the sound gets clearer.

110
00:08:06,265 --> 00:08:08,255
When the shape of your mouth changes,

111
00:08:08,255 --> 00:08:09,438
the sound changes too.

112
00:08:09,438 --> 00:08:11,750
Say today's words slowly, three times each.

113
00:08:15,940 --> 00:08:20,744
Next time we'll join the silent ㅇ to a vowel and build syllable blocks.

114
00:08:20,744 --> 00:08:23,451
See you soon! You did really well today.

115
00:08:23,451 --> 00:08:28,052
Isn't it surprising how much you can say without a single consonant?

116
00:08:28,052 --> 00:08:31,300
I'm off across the plaza now. See you next time!

